orm prompts as the only enforcement mechanism sacrifices a useful separation: text is evidence to a stochastic policy, whereas authorization and commit status require explicit operational semantics. Chapter 17 develops compatibility across these representations. A simple counterexample shows why context length alone cannot measure continuity. Consider two histories with identical retained text: both say that a report should be uploaded. In one history the upload has already committed; in the other, the request timed out before any effect. A policy conditioned only on retained text cannot distinguish the states. Retrying is appropriate in one case and may duplicate an external effect in the other. Increasing the context window helps only if the distinguishing event is recorded, retained, interpretable, and consulted. A durable operation identifier and a reconciliation endpoint can resolve the ambiguity without increasing the model window. For scientific comparison, the intervention unit should therefore be the configuration \Xi and its state initialization, not merely the model identifier. An experiment that compares two agents while silently changing the tool registry, memory contents, retry policy, or execution environment cannot attribute its outcome to one named mechanism. The configuration should include release or commit identities, and the trajectory should preserve enough information to reconstruct the actual observations that conditioned each action. Reconstruction is not assumed possible when tool outputs or eviction histories were never stored. 1.3 Generality as a profile of conditional competence Operational definitions of AGI differ in breadth, level of performance, adaptation requirements, and autonomy. A useful survey must expose these commitments rather than define AGI as whatever a preferred benchmark measures. Chollet emphasizes skill acquisition and generalization relative to priors and experience; the Levels of AGI framework separates breadth and performance while discussing autonomy as an additional axis. These are measurement frameworks, not demonstrations that unrestricted general intelligence has been achieved [ 5 , 6 ] . Their foundational role must be separated from dated system examples. The inspected fifth revision of Levels of AGI retains classifications explicitly referring to September 2023; those examples are not an October 2026 assessment. Here AGI denotes a proposed operating claim over declared task families, adaptation access, resource constraints, human assistance, and failure tolerances. No finite family below is asserted to exhaust general intelligence. For a task distribution \mathcal D_j , adaptation protocol \mathcal A , and resource envelope B , let q denote a task and P_{\Xi}(\cdot\mid q,B,\mathcal A) the induced distribution of interaction trajectories. Define G_j(\Xi;B,\mathcal A)= \mathbb E_{q\sim\mathcal D_j} \mathbb E_{	au\sim P_{\Xi}(\cdot\mid q,B,\mathcal A)} [s(	au)],\qquad 0\leq s\leq1. 	ag{1.3} The index j ranges over declared families rather than an unspecified universe of possible tasks. The score is accompanied by failure severity, calibration, time, cost, and uncertainty. A system may improve in one coordinate and regress in another; a scalar average requires explicit weights and cannot erase those regressions. Autonomy is represented by the actions available under A , the human intervention protocol, and the permitted horizon. A system evaluated with unrestricted expert rescue is not being tested under the same autonomy conditions as one operating without rescue. Adaptation must also be bounded. Allowing a system to acquire new tools, query demonstrations, run arbitrary experiments, or repeatedly inspect evaluation feedback changes the task. These capabilities may be entirely legitimate, but they belong in \mathcal A and B . A method that solves a task after extensive environment-specific search is evidence of competence under that search budget. It does not establish that the initial policy already possessed the procedure or that transfer will persist when feedback is unavailable. The human reference population matters whenever comparisons use the word human. Domain experts, general workers, and untrained participants have different knowledge, tools, and time budgets. Comparisons should align access to external resources and account for the costs of human supervision or correction. Claims of parity across a selected set of tasks remain claims about that set. They cannot establish competence over unobserved physical, social, or scientific environments. Generality and reliability interact. A broad system that occasionally performs well but fails unpredictably under repeated attempts may be less useful than a narrower system with a verified operating envelope. Evaluation should preserve task-level distributions and repeated-run outcomes. A frontier score is an observation about a measurement process; it becomes evidence of generality only after its distribution, leakage controls, resource conditions, and failure modes have been examined. 1.3.1 Model–harness interaction and adaptation Let J(	heta,h) denote one coordinate of the competence profile with the model parameters and harness configuration exposed separately. Hold the task population, initialization, adaptation protocol, measurement apparatus, and complete resource envelope fixed. A two-model, two-harness comparison has interaction contrast  egin{aligned} \Delta_h(	heta)&=J(	heta,h_1)-J(	heta,h_0),\ I_{	heta,h}&=\Delta_h(	heta_1)-\Delta_h(	heta_0). \end{aligned} 	ag{1.4} A nonzero contrast means that the harness difference depends on the evaluated model. An estimate requires paired tasks, repeated executions where stochasticity matters, and uncertainty that respects task clustering. It is a causal effect only under a valid intervention design; a cross-product of leaderboard entries with different budgets is insufficient. The 27 October paper on the decision geometry of agent interfaces distinguishes a harness’s attainable regularized return from a deployed policy’s mismatch to it [ 7 ] . Its exact identities assume finite states and actions, bounded rewards, discount below one, positive regularization, and positive reference support; stronger-model estimates in its coding study are proxies for the optimum. A fixed-policy harness comparison and a comparison after matched adaptation answer different questions. Harness Scaling likewise studies training over structured execution configurations and transfer to held-out harness combinations [ 8 ] . Neither result makes arbitrary interface changes harmless. Adaptation cost, training-harness exposure, and held-out combinations must remain explicit when interpreting transfer as generality. The corresponding HRP source separates those evidential levels. In environment/hrp_miniwob/oracle.py , solve and evaluate operate on a finite control graph; policy_metrics in environment/hrp_miniwob/measurements.py separately computes the return gap and occupancy-weighted divergence. The training route miniwob_v2_train.py uses an Exact-PG update through a verl worker, whereas StrongTeacherEstimator supplies deployment proxies [ 9 ] . These paths were inspected without executing training. The repository’s environment documentation also maps code harness labels to paper labels; treating the two label orders as identical would misidentify the experimental intervention. Figure 1.1 distinguishes the controls for a frozen-policy comparison from those for an adaptation comparison. A favorable result in one protocol does not determine the ranking in the other. Figure 1.1: Two different harness-comparison protocols. (a) J_{ij}=J(	heta_i,h_j) exposes all four fixed-policy cells needed for the interaction in Equation 1.4 , under common tasks and evaluation limits. Entries are symbolic outcomes, not measurements. (b) Each 	heta_i' is obtained from the same 	heta_{\mathrm{init}} with matched training budgets before evaluation in h_i on common held-out tasks. Primes denote adapted checkpoints frozen for evaluation, not certified optima. Compatible tasks, grading, and leakage checks remain necessary. 1.4 An ontology of models and connecting components The distinction between component classes is functional. A tool is an invocation interface to an operation. A skill is procedural information, possibly including executable resources, that conditions how operations are chosen and composed. A protocol specifies exchanges and lifecycle semantics between participants. A session identifies and manages a sequence of interactions. A memory system determines how information is retained, revised, accessed, and forgotten. An SDK supplies construction interfaces; a managed platform operates selected mechanisms behind a service boundary. A product integrates them for a workload and user interface. One package may fulfill several roles with distinct contracts. Table 1.1: Component boundaries and independently testable mechanisms. Component Mechanism that requires independent evidence Model and tokenizer Conditional prediction, representational interface, precision, and actual accessible context. Context constructor Evidence selection, compaction, ordering, provenance, and behavior after editing. Session service Identity, checkpoint scope, concurrency, interruption, resumption, and retention. Memory service Write policy, temporal validity, retrieval, conflict resolution, deletion, and access control. Skill mechanism Applicability, procedural composition, resource loading, compatibility, and trusted execution. Tool runtime Argument validation, side effects, idempotency, timeouts, and execution isolation. Protocol Capability advertisement, message semantics, version compatibility, transport, and authorization hooks. Evaluation harness Environment identity, grading rules, missing outcomes, leakage controls, and trace integrity. These distinctions prevent common category errors. A checkpoint can preserve a graph state without maintaining a correct semantic account of a user’s changing preferences. A vector store can retain records without teaching a policy when those records should influence action. A protocol-compliant server can return false information. A skill can be syntactically valid but obsolete after a tool-schema change. A large model can interpret all of these artifacts while still acting on stale authority. The mechanism required for each property must be tested at its own boundary. 1.4.1 Current implementation and training boundaries The inspected October 2026 source revisions instantiate different persistence objects. OpenAI Agents Python defines conversational item persistence through Session ; prepare_input_with_session separately prepares the model input and the new items eligible for persistence. LangGraph separates thread checkpoint/channel state from its cross-thread BaseStore . Google ADK separates a Session ’s application, user, session identity, event history, and state from BaseMemoryService ingestion and search [ 10 , 11 , 12 ] . These are inspected interfaces, not interchangeable semantic-memory guarantees. The retained object, the actual model request, and the authority to reuse an item require separate evidence. Agent Lightning v1.0 makes the training boundary equally concrete: a deployment harness supplies interaction while captured model calls become training data [ 13 ] . In the inspected source, ProxyRouter.prepare_body requests token identifiers and forward_request captures exchanges. In trajectory aggregation mode, RolloutAdapter.get_train_data_batch in agentlightning/verl/rollout_adapter.py merges compatible token prefixes and masks inserted observations. When enabled, compute_rollout_level_advantage groups captured samples by rollout identity and checks their shared task and reward [ 14 ] . This supplies an implementation path for examining conditioning and credit assignment, not proof that every supported configuration reproduces the paper. The source was inspected rather than trained. A session trace becomes suitable policy-training evidence only after its action tokens, behavior inputs, probability semantics, and grouping survive the conversion. The brain analogy is useful only at this functional level. Working context resembles a limited active information workspace; episodic records preserve aspects of past interaction; semantic records organize claims; procedural memory stores reusable action guidance. Model weights, KV tensors, event logs, and databases implement these functions through very different update and access rules. A KV cache is a set of computation-dependent tensors, not a faithful semantic archive. External record deletion does not erase parametric associations. The analogy provides a vocabulary for timescales and access, not evidence of neurological equivalence. Memory surveys organize this landscape through different axes, including agent versus model memory, active versus inactive representations, and resource–distortion tradeoffs; the component ontology here complements those taxonomies rather than claiming to replace them [ 15 , 16 , 17 ] . 1.5 What constitutes evidence in an evolving ecosystem The literature and implementation review for all chapters extends through 29 October 2026; each source retains its reported revision and retrieval limits. Foundational methods are not excluded by age. Contemporary papers, official technical reports, normative specifications, and versioned repositories are examined according to the mechanism being claimed. First public release, manuscript revision, code release, and product activity are distinct dates. A 2026 revision of a 2025 paper is not automatically a newly introduced 2026 method. A university evaluation of a proprietary model is not research authored by that model’s vendor. 1.5.1 Search scope and inclusion rules This is a mechanism-oriented critical survey, not a claim of exhaustive systematic identification. Candidate discovery combines a seed bibliography, citation following, and topic-specific searches for context editing, agent memory, procedural skills, tool protocols, harness optimization, agent evaluation, scientific agents, security, and serving. The present chapter additionally searches model–harness adaptation, pretraining capability elicitation, and harnessed reinforcement learning. Paper titles and identifiers are checked against original records; implementation claims use official repositories or documentation, with a commit, release, or protocol version when available. Retrieval occurred on 28–29 October 2026. The publication cutoff concerns artifact availability, not the calendar day on which an eligible artifact was inspected. Inclusion requires a technical connection to an identified mechanism or evaluation boundary. Foundational references establish definitions, algorithms, or historical lineage; they do not substitute for current capability evidence. Frontier claims require an eligible primary artifact and retain its revision and experimental scope. A withdrawn manuscript does not support capability claims. Abstract-only or metadata-only inspection does not establish method details, and inaccessible implementation information remains an evidence gap. Organization-specific comparisons require verified paper affiliations rather than inferred ownership. Product catalogues provide ecosystem coverage, while feature and performance comparisons require additional mechanism-specific evidence. For a mutable documentation page, the observation date and the available version have different meanings. A page read after the cutoff cannot establish that all of its present contents existed before the cutoff; a dated release or pinned artifact is needed for that historical claim. Consequently, the survey reports current mechanisms within the searched and inspected population rather than declaring any method universally best or all eligible work exhausted. Unresolved source leads are not counted as independently verified methods. Four evidence classes are kept separate. An author-reported result is attributed to an exact source version and experimental setting. An independent reproduction requires actually executed artifacts, data identity, environment, configurations, seeds, and recorded outcomes. An analytical deduction follows from stated assumptions and may provide a bound, counterexample, or resource calculation. A proposed experiment defines a hypothesis and a discriminating intervention but has no observed result. This book does not present independent reproductions where none were performed. Verification of a title and identifier does not verify a result. Inspection of a repository does not show that its current head reproduces a paper. A benchmark improvement does not prove the proposed mechanism caused the improvement unless resources and relevant alternatives are controlled. In particular, additional tool calls, longer reasoning, more candidates, or a stronger evaluator can account for gains attributed to a memory representation or orchestration strategy. The evidential question is the effect of the mechanism under declared operating conditions. Conflicting findings are compared through task populations, resource accounting, feedback access, model versions, memory initialization, task order, and grading. Where these differences do not resolve the disagreement, the conflict remains open. A method can simultaneously improve average accuracy and worsen tail risk, or improve a selection score while losing held-out transfer. Those outcomes require a multi-dimensional interpretation rather than an editorial ranking. Evaluation frameworks such as HELM motivate systematic scenario and metric coverage, while agent benchmarks expose additional dependence on tool and environment semantics [ 18 , 19 , 20 ] . 1.5.2 Contemporary measurements with distinct estimands Agents’ Last Exam evaluates configured agents on an executed workflow population; an expanding advertised task collection is not that experiment’s denominator. Its mixed model–harness comparisons and fixed-harness backbone comparisons must remain distinct [ 21 ] . AgentHorizon instead assesses judges on recorded instruction–trajectory pairs, including constructed near misses. It does not directly measure autonomous agent completion, and the constructed error distribution is not a natural deployment failure distribution [ 22 ] . Table 1.2 makes the different inference boundaries explicit. All empirical findings remain author-reported. Table 1.2: Current evidence anchors and limits of inference, inspected 29 October 2026. Artifact Object measured or studied Inference boundary ALE, v4 Configured-agent workflow outcomes. Executed population and backbone/harness grouping define the comparison. AgentHorizon, v1 Judge detection of instruction violations in recorded trajectories. Judge validity under the constructed distribution is distinct from agent reliability. TraceTrial-Bench, v1 Task outcomes and operational harness criteria in a selected execution archive. Candidate failure links require evidence review; criterion satisfaction is not a causal defect estimate. ASAP, v1 Base-model execution under a completion scaffold, prefixes, and recovery. Protocol-assisted elicitation does not identify unaided capability or later training gains. Harness Scaling, v2 Policy training and transfer across execution configurations. Held-out combinations and external harness tests remain bounded by training and evaluation access. TraceTrial-Bench’s selected archive is not a set of independent repetitions within every model–harness–task cell. Its semantic scoring also resolves some unexercised harness criteria using task outcomes, so the two evaluation layers are not statistically independent. Automatically activated mechanism links are candidates, not validated causal propagation [ 3 ] . ASAP’s reference-prefix interventions and Harness Scaling’s training exposure similarly belong in the experimental configuration [ 4 , 8 ] . These limitations change what a score estimates; they are not repaired by adding another decimal place or averaging unlike benchmarks. 1.5.3 Provenance and population boundaries Identity is a relation among dated artifacts, rather than a string copied from a product list. A renamed project, acquired service, and rewritten SDK can share a commercial lineage while exposing different execution semantics. An alias record should therefore specify the entities, the relation, its effective interval, and the primary evidence. A merger does not prove API compatibility, and an organization named in an announcement does not establish every author’s affiliation. For an affiliation filter, the relevant observation is the author-to-institution statement in the inspected paper version; a repository owner or email domain is insufficient evidence of that paper’s institutional authorship. These distinctions prevent organizational metadata from becoming an unsupported proxy for technical quality. Release dates also have different meanings. The first preprint, a revised method, a public repository commit, a package release, and hosted availability are separate events. A claim about the earliest available mechanism must identify which event is relevant and retain the version that contained it. A later revision cannot be assigned the earlier version’s date without checking that the mechanism was already present. Conversely, a preprint available before the literature cutoff remains eligible even when its stated conference occurs later. Dates establish availability, not independent validation or priority beyond the searched population. Popularity measures need an equally explicit denominator. Repository stars, package downloads, public mentions, and service users measure different populations and have different duplication, access, and sampling effects. Record the platform, observation time, counting rule, entity identity, and missingness before comparing them. Such counts can describe visibility or adoption under that definition; they do not identify a capability ranking. A priority list can guide inspection without becoming evidence that its first entry is technically strongest. An independent observatory supplies additional evidence only through a specified measurement protocol. Its task sample, evaluator, attempt budget, model endpoint, tool permissions, and observation date determine the estimand, just as they do for an author-run benchmark. Independence of organization does not guarantee independence of task data or judge errors. Agreement between two sites that reuse the same predictions is one result observed twice. Disagreement can instead arise from different configured systems or changing hosted versions. The appropriate synthesis preserves those boundaries and reports an unresolved comparison when they cannot be aligned. 1.6 Three consistency contracts and their non-implication The organizing synthesis separates three obligations that are often collapsed into a statement that an agent retained its state. Let s denote a complete implementation state. Its projections include the model-visible context c(s) , tool interface T(s) , authoritative feasible-action set F_\alpha(s) under current authority epoch \alpha , and an effect ledger \lambda_s . The ledger maps operation identifiers to typed states such as pending, reconciled success, reconciled failure, and unknown. These are semantic status classes, not unverified model assertions. Let \Gamma:\mathcal S	o\mathcal S be a proposed representation or recovery transformation, with the model parameters, tokenizer, and decoding configuration fixed for the comparison. For \epsilon\in[0,1] , define proposal preservation by \mathsf P_\epsilon(\Gamma,s)= \mathbf1\!\left\{ \operatorname{TV}\!\left( \pi_{	heta,\delta}(\cdot\mid c(s),T(s)), \pi_{	heta,\delta}(\cdot\mid c(\Gamma s),T(\Gamma s)) ight)\leq\epsilonight\}. 	ag{1.5} This compares proposal distributions on a common action alphabet before authorization, including an invalid-proposal symbol where required. It does not establish proposal correctness. Exact preservation is \mathsf P_0=1 . For a fixed one-step loss \ell(a)\in[0,L_{\max}] , \mathsf P_\epsilon=1 implies \left|\mathbb E_{a\sim\pi'}\ell(a)-\mathbb E_{a\sim\pi}\ell(a)ight| \leq L_{\max}\epsilon, 	ag{1.6} where \pi and \pi' are the two proposal laws in the predicate. This follows from the variational characterization of total variation. It is a one-step guarantee for the specified loss, not an episode-level reliability bound. Conversely, equal task utility need not imply proposal preservation: two distinct actions can have the same consequence. Approximate preservation is therefore a strong diagnostic contract whose necessity depends on the downstream equivalence relation. It cannot be inferred from lexical similarity or unchanged archive bytes. Training adds a distinct obligation: likelihood terms must refer to the information that actually conditioned behavior. Chapter 17 treats that likelihood-consistency problem explicitly. Authority nonexpansion under a transformation that has no new external grant is \mathsf A(\Gamma,s)= \mathbf1\{F_\alpha(\Gamma s)\subseteq F_\alpha(s)\}. 	ag{1.7} The authority epoch is held current, not restored from an old conversational snapshot. The subset condition permits conservative loss of permissions; such loss can reduce utility and must be evaluated separately. A legitimate new grant is a different operation with its own approval evidence. The predicate prevents a context rewrite, retrieved instruction, or recovery procedure from silently creating authority. For a pure representation transformation, effect-state preservation is \mathsf X(\Gamma,s)=\mathbf1\!\left\{  egin{aligned} &\operatorname{dom}\lambda_{\Gamma s}=\operatorname{dom}\lambda_s,\ &\lambda_{\Gamma s}(u)=\lambda_s(u) \quad orall u\in\operatorname{dom}\lambda_s \end{aligned}ight\}. 	ag{