trates durable effect-ledger reconstruction [ 25 , 22 ] . The originality of this survey lies in analyzing those boundaries jointly, connecting their assumptions to identifiable implementations, and constructing interventions that distinguish their failures. It does not relabel context editing, rate-distortion analysis, privilege control, or durable execution as newly discovered ideas. Contemporary memory and compression surveys already organize substantial parts of that landscape [ 15 , 16 , 17 ] . The added question is which contracts survive composition and co-adaptation, and what evidence can falsify that claim. Chapters 17 , 29 , 32 , and 33 develop the resulting conditioning, authority, recovery, and integration analyses. Figure 1.2 distinguishes the information, authority, and effect boundaries used by these predicates. Figure 1.2: Three separate state contracts. (a) An illustrative history–context–policy path under fixed parameters and decoding; memory and other constructor inputs are omitted. (b) Current grants at authority epoch \alpha gate permitted actions and no dispatch. (c) With \lambda=\lambda_s and \lambda'=\lambda_{\Gamma s} , a pure representation transformation preserves operation identities and recorded status, including unknown outcomes. Reconciliation with new service evidence is a different transition. The schematic does not establish task correctness. 1.6.1 From preservation contracts to corrective evidence A state transformation can preserve every proposal, permission, and ledger entry while preserving a wrong policy or an incorrect historical record. The three contracts therefore govern transformation integrity; task acceptance requires additional observed evidence. An operational implementation should return scoped verdicts and identify which evidence can resolve a failed or undecidable check. Merely adding a verifier whose output is always accepted would leave this distinction empty. Let \mathfrak G\subseteq\{\Gamma:\mathcal S	o\mathcal 